WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.301
Every control that blocks an administrative
path also creates a way to lock the

2
00:00:05.301 --> 00:00:08.244
agency out of its own
tenant: federation down,

3
00:00:08.244 --> 00:00:12.938
every administrator's MFA device
unavailable, a misconfigured policy,

4
00:00:12.938 --> 00:00:17.075
or every privileged role eligible
with no approver able to act.

5
00:00:17.075 --> 00:00:20.428
Emergency access accounts,
break-glass, are the answer,

6
00:00:20.428 --> 00:00:24.750
and they're the only standing privilege
the program accepts by design.

7
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.500
The standard is on the left.

8
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:31.593
Two or more cloud-only accounts on the
tenant's onmicrosoft dot com domain, neither

9
00:00:31.593 --> 00:00:36.312
synchronized nor federated, and not tied
to one person or an employee's phone.

10
00:00:36.312 --> 00:00:41.146
Permanent active Global Administrator;
together with service principals that can't

11
00:00:41.146 --> 00:00:45.775
perform an activation, they're the only
standing active assignments in the tenant.

12
00:00:45.775 --> 00:00:50.130
Phishing-resistant authentication,
a FIDO2 passkey or certificate-based

13
00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:54.825
authentication, using methods different
from the normal administrator accounts.

14
00:00:54.825 --> 00:00:59.194
The older pattern of a password in
sealed envelopes is superseded,

15
00:00:59.194 --> 00:01:02.371
because a password alone
can no longer reach the

16
00:01:02.371 --> 00:01:06.025
admin portals where Microsoft's
mandatory MFA applies.

17
00:01:06.025 --> 00:01:10.003
Excluded from every policy that
blocks or restricts sign-in,

18
00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:11.913
through one dedicated group.

19
00:01:11.913 --> 00:01:14.986
Validated at least every
ninety days by signing

20
00:01:14.986 --> 00:01:17.675
in and performing an administrative task.

21
00:01:17.675 --> 00:01:19.825
On the right is how
the build watches them.

22
00:01:19.825 --> 00:01:23.254
Report-only policies
don't need the exclusion,

23
00:01:23.254 --> 00:01:27.225
so CA090 is the one policy
that records their sign-ins.

24
00:01:27.225 --> 00:01:29.578
Alerts match the accounts' object IDs,

25
00:01:29.578 --> 00:01:33.450
because their names deliberately
don't reveal their purpose.

26
00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:38.138
Every use is a critical incident
until a post-use review closes it.

27
00:01:38.138 --> 00:01:43.685
And because NIST's AC-2(2) expects emergency
accounts to be disabled automatically

28
00:01:43.685 --> 00:01:48.836
after a defined period, the system security
plan records these as a documented

29
00:01:48.836 --> 00:01:50.658
deviation, with monitoring,

30
00:01:50.658 --> 00:01:55.413
post-use review and the ninety-day
validation as compensating controls.

31
00:01:55.413 --> 00:01:56.799
Finally, order.

32
00:01:56.799 --> 00:02:00.375
The accounts exist and pass
a test before any role

33
00:02:00.375 --> 00:02:03.775
is made eligible or any
approval is switched on.

34
00:02:03.775 --> 00:02:07.080
Module three explains the
lockout that prevents.
